messages:
lauren: Quick check — planning to add prod S3 access for PartnerX. Proposed flow: partner -> partner-proxy NAT -> our VPC -> S3 (gateway VPCE). I opened a Terraform PR: https://github.com/redwood/infra/pull/4821. Need confirmation on: 1) allowed source IPs on our proxy, 2) bucket policy restrictions (sourceVpce vs IPs), and 3) what we should log (we must avoid storing object keys or PII). Terraform snippet from PR below.

lauren: ```hcl
resource "aws_vpc_endpoint" "s3_prod" {
  vpc_id         = "vpc-0abc1234"
  service_name   = "com.amazonaws.us-east-1.s3"
  route_table_ids = [aws_route_table.private.id]
  policy = <<POLICY
{
  "Statement":[
    {"Effect":"Allow","Principal":"*","Action":"s3:*","Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::redwood-partnerx/*","Condition":{"StringEquals":{"aws:sourceVpce":"vpce-0abcd1234"}}}
  ]
}
POLICY
}
```

sam: Heads up: S3 endpoints are gateway-type (route table) so security groups won't apply. If partner traffic comes through their NAT/proxy and then our proxy, we'll need to allowlist their egress IPs on our proxy/firewall, or front them with an internal NLB with mTLS.

nirav: Quick security notes:
- For S3 prefer restricting by aws:sourceVpce in bucket policy when possible (gives a strong network boundary). If partner can't use our VPC, then source IP allowlist is the fallback.
- We should also restrict by aws:SourceAccount or explicit principal to avoid wide-open access.
- Logging: do NOT persist raw object keys or any headers that may contain PII (e.g., X-User-Email). Store only request_id, bucket, operation, bytes, and a hashed object key if you absolutely need to correlate.

nirav: Example bucket policy condition (add to PR):

```json
{
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Deny",
    "Principal": "*",
    "Action": "s3:*",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::redwood-partnerx/*",
    "Condition": {
      "StringNotEquals": { "aws:sourceVpce": "vpce-0abcd1234" }
    }
  }]
}
```

lauren: Partner says they cannot guarantee static egress IPs from their environment. Proposed mitigation: front partner with an internal NLB + mTLS client certs (they present cert, we terminate at our proxy). Is mTLS acceptable from security side? Also who needs to sign off on the PR for the bucket policy/logging changes?

julia: mTLS + NLB is acceptable; adds much stronger assurance than IP allowlists. If partner can't provide static IPs, go that route. For signoff: tag @eng-security and @eng-infra in the PR; Nirav can do the security signoff once conditions & logging are updated.

devsec-bot: Automated checklist update: [bucket-policy-restricts-vpce: false] [source-ip-allowlist-provided: false] [mTLS-configured: pending] [log-redaction-config: missing] PR checks: infra-lint ✅, tfplan ⚠️ (policy change detected).

nirav: I'll review the PR tonight. One request: add a small log-redact config in the infra module so it's explicit. Example (pseudo):

```hcl
locals {
  log_redact_rules = {
    mask_headers = ["X-User-Email"]
    hash_fields  = ["s3.object.key"]
  }
}

module "s3_logging" {
  source = "//modules/logging"
  redact_rules = local.log_redact_rules
}
```

nirav: Also include a short runbook entry for incident ops describing where the original object key can be recovered (if ever) and who can request it (requires ticket + legal/ops approval).

lauren: Thanks — will update the PR with the VPCE bucket condition, add the redact rules, and open INFRA-412 to track mTLS cert exchange. I'll ping you both when ready for review.

sam: LGTM — thanks for moving fast. :thumbsup:
